# tests/backrefs/caseless.rxt -- module `backrefs` ([M6.5.2]): WHERE the `(?i)` is read and WHAT the compare folds (S4)
#
# GENERATED BY tests/backrefs/gen_corpus.py, and that is a property rather
# than a convenience: every expectation below was produced by driving the cell
# through libpcre2 10.46 (via the committed ctypes binding at
# docs/design/backrefs_measurements/probes/br_oracle.py) BEFORE it was written.
# python3 `re` was driven over the SAME cells in the same pass, and a block
# carries `# pcre2-only` exactly where python diverged or could not compile the
# pattern -- DETECTED, never assumed, with the first divergence and the cell
# count recorded above the marking.
#
# THAT MARKING IS THE WHOLE REASON THE GENERATOR EXISTS. This module has the
# largest oracle divergence pcrec has met: python `re` refuses EVERY
# self-reference and EVERY forward reference at compile time, has no `\g`, no
# `\k` and no `(?J)` at all, rejects the `(?<n>...)` declaring spelling, and
# refuses `(?i)` anywhere but the pattern start -- which is exactly where the
# two cells that decide WHERE the caseless flag is read live. R32 C3 found the
# first test plan marking two files python-verifiable in the direction that
# LOSES the oracle; computing the marking removes the species.
#
# Design: docs/design/backrefs_design.md. Divergences are catalogued in
# docs/dev/upstream_issues.md.

# ====================================================================
# THE CASELESSNESS IS THE OPTION IN FORCE AT THE REFERENCE (§3.1(c))
# ====================================================================

# F7's positive half: the reference is inside `(?i:...)`, so the COMPARE folds and "aA" matches. `Ast.caseless` is set from the scoped state AT THE BACKREFERENCE, exactly as `Ast.multiline` is set at the `$`. Sabotage S-BR2 ignores the field and this cell is what sees it.
pattern ^(a)(?i:\1)$
features backrefs,modifiers
m "aA" 0 2
g 1 0 1
m "aa" 0 2
g 1 0 1
n "Aa"

# F7's negative half, and the one a plausible implementation gets wrong: the `(?i)` is at the GROUP, not at the reference, so the compare is CASE-SENSITIVE and "aA" does NOT match. An implementation reading the option in force at the group -- or at end of pattern -- passes the cell above and fails this one.
pattern ^(?i:(a))\1$
features backrefs,modifiers
n "aA"
m "aa" 0 2
g 1 0 1
n "Aa"
m "AA" 0 2
g 1 0 1

# python REFUSES this pattern outright ("global flags not at the start of the expression"), so one of §3.1(c)'s two load-bearing cells has no python oracle at all. R32 C3 found the first test plan calling this file python-verifiable.
# python DIVERGES here, measured rather than assumed: "aA" @0 pcre2=('n',) python=('ERR', 'global flags not at the start of the expression at position 2') (3 of 3 cells)
# pcre2-only
pattern ^((?i)a)\1$
features backrefs,modifiers
n "aA"
m "aa" 0 2
g 1 0 1
m "AA" 0 2
g 1 0 1

# python DIVERGES here, measured rather than assumed: "aA" @0 pcre2=('n',) python=('ERR', 'global flags not at the start of the expression at position 1') (2 of 2 cells)
# pcre2-only
pattern ^(?i)(a)(?-i)\1$
features backrefs,modifiers
n "aA"
m "aa" 0 2
g 1 0 1

# ====================================================================
# WHAT FOLDS: exactly the 52 ASCII letters, and nothing else
# ====================================================================

# AXIS A, measured over all 256 bytes against libpcre2's 8-bit NON-UTF build: the compare folds EXACTLY the 52 ASCII letters, each with one partner, and NO non-ASCII byte folds. 0xdf (sharp s) is one of the three the design names.
pattern ^(\xdf)\1$
features backrefs,modifiers
flags i
m "\xdf\xdf" 0 2

# THE CELL THE SIGNATURE IS DESIGNED FOR. In an 8-bit build every fold pair is one byte to one byte, so the compare cannot change length and "ss\xdf" does NOT match. A UTF-8 backend would have to answer this family differently -- one captured character folding to two -- which is why the residual entry returns a LENGTH rather than a bool: the shared emitter never computes one, it only adds the one it is given.
pattern ^(ss)\1$
features backrefs,modifiers
flags i
m "ssss" 0 4
n "ss\xdf"

pattern ^(a)\1$
features backrefs
flags i
m "aA" 0 2
g 1 0 1
m "Aa" 0 2
g 1 0 1
m "aa" 0 2
g 1 0 1
m "AA" 0 2
g 1 0 1

pattern ^(\w+)\1$
features backrefs,classes
flags i
m "AbcaBC" 0 6
g 1 0 3
n "Abcabd"

pattern ^(\xb5)\1$
features backrefs
flags i
m "\xb5\xb5" 0 2

pattern ^(\xff)\1$
features backrefs
flags i
m "\xff\xff" 0 2
